s with two or more colours. Examples:\n  - Pair 1: the 1/1/1/4/1 shape, and the 3/8 shape.\n  - Pair 2: the 2/4/8 shape.\n  - Pair 3: the 1/2/3 shape.\n- **Fragments** are the components with only one colour. Examples:\n  - Pair 1: a single 4, and a diagonal pair of 8s.\n  - Pair 2: a lone 4 and a lone 8. Diagonal adjacency doesn't join them because they are 2 cells apart, so both are fragments. They line up with the template's 4 and 8 at the same offset (7,4).\n  - Pair 3: two diagonal pairs of 2s.\n\n**How the output is built.** Each fragment is the same as part of a template, in the same orientation with no rotation, only shifted. I take the template cell of the fragment's colour and work out the shift that lines the fragment up with it. Then I check that every fragment cell lands on a template cell of the same colour. If it does, I stamp the whole template at that shift. The shifts I matched:\n- Pair 1: the 4 shifts the 1-shape by (7,10), and the 8-pair pulls the 3/8 shape up to (3,11).\n- Pair 2: the 4 and the 8 each match at offset (7,4), so they produce the same stamp (stamping it twice changes nothing).\n- Pair 3: each 2-pair receives the full 1/2/3 template.\n\n**One extra detail (pair 3).** In pair 3 the original template loses its 3 cell: (4,3) becomes background. Colour 3 is the only colour in any train input that appears exactly once in the whole grid. So I erase input cells whose colour occurs exactly once, before stamping; the stamped copies still carry the 3. I checked the counts:\n- Pairs 1 and 2: no colour occurs exactly once, so nothing is erased.\n- Both test inputs: no colour occurs exactly once either, so this rule only matters for pair 3.\n\n**Test inputs.**\n- Test 1:\n  - The lone 2s get the 5-ring that has a 2 in the centre.\n  - The lone 4 gets the 6-shape that has a 4 in the middle.\n  - The diagonal 3-pair gets the 1/3/9 shape.\n- Test 2:\n  - The lone 1s get the 8-shape that contains a 1.\n  - The diagonal 2-pairs get the 4/2/3/8 shape, matched through its diagonal 2s.\n\n**Stages emitted.** The program emits:\n1. the canvas after any erase (only if a cell was actually erased);\n2. one canvas after each template is stamped;\n3. the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    counts = {c: int((g == c).sum()) for c in np.unique(g)}\n    comps = components(g != bg, diagonal=True)\n    templates, frags = [], []\n    for comp in comps:\n        cols = set(int(g[y, x]) for y, x in comp)\n        (templates if len(cols) > 1 else frags).append(comp)\n    # erase colours occurring exactly once (template marker quirk)\n    erased = False\n    for c, n in counts.items():\n        if c != bg and n == 1:\n            out[g == c] = bg\n            erased = True\n    if erased:\n        emit(out.copy(), \"Erase the cell whose colour occurs only once in the grid.\")\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for fr in frags:\n        fy, fx = fr[0]\n        fc = int(g[fy, fx])\n        done = False\n        for t in templates:\n            tmap = {(y, x): int(g[y, x]) for y, x in t}\n            for (ty, tx), c in tmap.items():\n                if c != fc:\n                    continue\n                dy, dx = fy - ty, fx - tx\n                if all(tmap.get((y - dy, x - dx)) == int(g[y, x]) for y, x in fr):\n                    for (y, x), cc in tmap.items():\n                        ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n                        if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W:\n                            out[ny, nx] = cc\n                    emit(out.copy(), f\"Fragment of colour {fc} at ({fy},{fx}) matches a template; stamp the full template around it.\")\n                    done = True\n                    break\n            if done:\n                break\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All fragments completed into full templates.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Group the non-background cells into 8-connected components (diagonal neighbours count)."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Fragments** are the components with only one colour."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":".** Each fragment is the same as part of a template, in the same orientation with no rotation, only shifted."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I take the template cell of the fragment's colour and work out the shift that lines the fragment up with it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"If it does, I stamp the whole template at that shift."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Every fragment is a piece of one template, and the output rebuilds the whole template around it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They line up with the template's 4 and 8 at the same offset (7,4)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the output is built.** Each fragment is the same as part of a template, in the same orientation with no rotation, only shifted."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":" grid is made of.** Group the non-background cells into 8-connected components (diagonal neighbours count)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I checked the counts:"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"7dcf3044452a057f91b9a9a8d49d79b6e005d3a0de8a87fc227e81fcd84b97d5","task_sha256":"9d5c3d98f5a90e19eb7bcf946d516000ab3c4fa27b5958b4a2c882b7769cfe7f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
